("v4290", "type", "String")
("v4290", "scalar", "Skip to content\nAaron Alderman\nQuestions\nThreads\nAtlas / Map\n\nQUESTIONS / CHRISTIAN\n\nHow do I actually\nlive like Jesus?\n\nHere. With these people. With the power I have.\n\nI’m building software, pursuing an account of the physical world, and trying to follow Christ. I want those commitments to answer to one another—in the way I work, listen and care.\n\nExplore a situation in The Field →\nFollow the questions ↓\n\nThe question becomes real in what I do next.\n\nAN OPEN HAND. A REAL RESPONSIBILITY.\n\nFaith has to meet the person in front of me.\n\nI want following Christ to become visible in how I pay attention, share power and remain answerable. Sometimes that means helping. Sometimes it means a boundary that protects someone else.\n\n01 / ATTEND\n\nSee the person.\n\nListen before I turn someone’s situation into my explanation. Separate what I know from what I am assuming.\n\n02 / MAKE ROOM\n\nKeep a choice open.\n\nOffer help without making submission its price. Accept a cost freely, and ask who else is being asked to carry it.\n\n03 / ANSWER FOR IT\n\nStay open to repair.\n\nGive reasons. Hear an objection. Protect people from harm, and change course when the consequences call for it.\n\nA Christian starting point: Philippians 2:3–8 · John 13:1–17 · Luke 10:25–37\n\nExplore power, service and restraint →\n\nFAITH / PHYSICS / SOFTWARE / THE WORK OF CARE\n\nQuestions I have to answer.\n\nIf my tools, explanations and beliefs ask for trust, I owe people ways to question them. These are some of the places where that responsibility becomes concrete.\n\n01 / POWER & AGENCY\n\nWhere am I helping—and where am I taking over?\n\nHaving the power to act does not settle what I owe the person in front of me. I need to ask whose freedom my help makes possible.\n\nExplore power and agency →\n\n02 / ENGINEERING & RESTRAINT\n\nWhat must I refuse, even if I can make it work?\n\nA working system can still ask too much of people. I want my engineering to make its authority, limits and costs visible.\n\nExamine permission and responsibility →\n\n03 / PHYSICS & TRUTH\n\nWhat would make me surrender the theory I want to be true?\n\nIf I ask an explanation to earn my trust, I have to leave it somewhere to fail. That includes the explanations I most want to succeed.\n\nEnter the working laboratory →\n\n04 / SOURCERY & ACCOUNTABILITY\n\nCan the software I build answer for what it does?\n\nPeople should be able to follow a change, question its reasons and retain a meaningful say in what happens next.\n\nFollow the human question in Sourcery →\n\n05 / ATTENTION & CARE\n\nHow do I keep caring when understanding isn’t enough?\n\nI can understand a principle and still miss the person. I want to practise attention, listen to correction and keep finding contact.\n\nBegin with care →\n\n06 / HISTORY & REPAIR\n\nHow do I repair harm without rewriting what happened?\n\nAn apology cannot make the past disappear. Repair has to leave room for truth, changed practice and the other person’s boundaries.\n\nFollow the visible seam →\nTHE FIELD / A PLACE TO PRACTISE\n\nWHERE THE QUESTION MEETS A SITUATION\n\nSomeone asks for help. What do I owe them?\n\nThe Field begins with a workshop shift that needs covering. A private request. An uncertain reason. A problem that will still exist tomorrow.\n\nI want to examine what a choice does now, what it teaches a relationship, and what it makes normal over time. You can bring a different judgment—and ask what the scenario leaves out.\n\nEnter the workshop encounter →\n\nOne fictional encounter. Five choices. Room to disagree.\n\nExplore a household over time →\n\nURGENCY WITHOUT TAKING OVER\n\nWhat is mine to do next?\n\nI cannot wait until every explanation is complete to practise care. I can act with reasons, acknowledge what I don’t know, and remain responsible for repair.\n\nRead why this is personal →\nFollow a thread →\nExplore the atlas →\nSourcerer →\nAuthor →\nDad →\nChristian →\nQuestions image assurance\n\nIllustrations fill their frames; emblems fit inside them. Loading, image dimensions and card bounds are observed below; fitting is checked at source. This diagnostic copy uses eager loading. Artistic crops remain visual review.\n\n6 images observed · 0 failed checks. One image per card.\n\nillustration / cover · 92 × 130 · loaded · consistent frame · inside card\nemblem / contain · 92 × 130 · loaded · consistent frame · inside card\nillustration / cover · 92 × 130 · loaded · consistent frame · inside card\nemblem / contain · 92 × 130 · loaded · consistent frame · inside card\nillustration / cover · 92 × 130 · loaded · consistent frame · inside card\nillustration / cover · 92 × 130 · loaded · consistent frame · inside card\n\nAll image diagnostics\n\nAaron Alderman\n\nA few things I’ve built. A few things I’m still trying to understand.\n\nGitHub ↗\nLinkedIn ↗\nBuilt with Worldwright.io ↗\nAssurance\nLicence ↗\nChristian stewardship covenant ↗")
